\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The determinant as a rooted-forest sum}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{A \newvocab{rooted spanning forest} on #{V} is an acyclic set
#{F\subseteq E} together with exactly one chosen vertex in each
component, including isolated vertices. Write #{R} for these roots
and #{r=|R|}. Loops cannot occur, and distinct parallel labels count
as distinct choices. With the data of \ref{fdip-005R},
##{\det(m^2I-L_w)
 =\sum_{(F,R)}\left(\prod_{e\in F}w_e\right)m^{2|R|}.}
Normalize these nonnegative terms to obtain a probability law on
rooted spanning forests. It satisfies
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathbb P(e\in F)&=w_e\langle d_e,Gd_e\rangle,\\
\mathbb E[|R|]&=m^2\operatorname{tr}G,\\
\mathbb E[|F|]&=n-m^2\operatorname{tr}G.
\end{aligned}}
If the positive-weight support is connected, with weighted
spanning-tree sum #{\tau_w}, then
##{\det(m^2I-L_w)=n\tau_wm^2+O(m^4)
 \qquad(m\downarrow0).}
Thus the spanning-tree coefficient appears after division by
#{m^2}; the unscaled determinant tends to zero.}

\proof{
\p{Order the vertices and the labelled edges, and use the counting
bases of the \vocabk{counting inner products}{fdip-0002}.
Form the #{n\times(|E|+n)} matrix whose
columns are the coordinates of #{\sqrt{w_e}d_e} for edges and of
#{m\delta_v} for vertices.
Call it #{A}; then #{AA^T=m^2I-L_w}. Cauchy-Binet expresses
its determinant as the sum of the squared #{n\times n} minors.
A chosen minor consists of edge columns #{F} and vertex columns
#{R}, with #{|F|+|R|=n}. A cycle in #{F} makes its incidence
columns dependent (this includes a loop and the two-edge cycle
of parallel edges), so only forests contribute.}

\p{Suppose #{F} is a forest with components #{C_i}. Their
incidence columns span the coordinate-sum-zero subspace in each
#{\mathbb R^{C_i}}, of dimension #{|C_i|-1}. This follows by
successively removing leaves. A component with no chosen vertex
column therefore prevents full rank. There are as many chosen
vertex columns as components, because
#{|R|=n-|F|}; hence full rank requires exactly one in each
component. Conversely, for a tree with one root, the matrix of its
unweighted incidence columns and that root's coordinate vector
has determinant #{\pm1}: remove a nonroot leaf and expand along
its row, then finish with the root's #{1\times1} matrix.
Applying this to all components proves that precisely the stated
forests contribute, with squared minor
#{(\prod_{e\in F}w_e)m^{2|R|}}. Zero-weight edge columns
vanish, consistent with deletion.}

\p{Put #{t=m^2} and call this polynomial #{D(w,t)}.
Termwise differentiation gives
#{w_e\partial_eD=\sum_{F\ni e,R}(\prod_{f\in F}w_f)t^{|R|}}
and #{t\partial_tD=\sum_{F,R}|R|(\prod_{f\in F}w_f)t^{|R|}}.
Divide by #{D>0} and use the determinant derivative from
\ref{fdip-005S}, first in direction #{d_e\otimes d_e^\flat}, then in
direction #{I}. The edge identity holds also at #{w_e=0}, when
both sides vanish. Finally every forest has #{|F|+|R|=n}.
For connected positive support, the coefficient of #{t} comes
from spanning trees; each has #{n} choices of root. All other
terms have at least two roots and hence order #{t^2}.}
}

\p{Equivalently, add a new boundary vertex #{*} with an edge of
weight #{m^2} to each old vertex. Attach #{*} to the chosen root
of each forest component; this is a bijection to spanning trees
of the augmented graph. Its Dirichlet Laplacian at #{*} is
#{m^2I-L_w}. This is the trivial-connection case of Kenyon's
boundary theorem: cycle factors vanish, leaving trees rooted at
the boundary \citet{Theorem 7, Section 8, p. 15}{kenyon2011spanning}.
The conductance extension is given in
\citet{Section 7.1, Theorem 5, p. 13}{kenyon2011spanning}.
The proof above includes the needed weights and zero-weight faces.
Chaiken's all-minors matrix-tree theorem enumerates directed
forests by the minors of a Laplacian whose columns sum to zero
\citet{pp. 319-329}{chaiken1982minors}. The expansion proved
above is the undirected weighted case, obtained from
Cauchy-Binet. It does not reproduce Chaiken's directed sign rule.}

\p{The Cauchy-Binet provider boundary, for a matrix over a
commutative ring with rows indexed by a finite ordered set, is
\ref{fdip-00LR}.}
